From 153e11c0e98614475a9c45734c9bc6bee4897c8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:48:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers, settings: the saturation of bitmap subtitles
 (subtitles.hadesbitmapsaturation)

Some find the colours of picture-based subtitles too vivid, the yellow of some 4K discs most of all,
and other builds offer their saturation beside their brightness. Hades had the brightness
(subtitles.hadesbitmapbrightness); "Bitmap subtitle saturation" is now beside it (Player >
Subtitles, at the same level): from 100 %, the default, which leaves the colours as they were made
(nothing is computed: the same bytes), down to 0 %, the grey of their luma, in steps of 5. As the
light, it is taken in linear light and around the colour's own luma, BT.709 for an sRGB colour and
BT.2020 for one in PQ (AdjustColour(), OverlayColour.h), so a paler colour keeps its brightness; with
the light below 100 % too, both are applied in one step. PGS, VobSub (DVD) and a bitmap subtitle in
ARGB; disc menus, DVB and text subtitles are not changed. A change makes the textures of the
subtitles again, as one of the light does.

Texts in en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx (core 39232-39233, read from their Estuary twins 32301-32302
by the settings dialog).

Tests (outside the tree): pgs_colour_test, AdjustColour(): at 100 % ScaleLight() byte for byte for
every colour; at 0 % a grey of the colour's luma in both curves; the luma kept within a code; against
an independent computation in double; and the palette path, the ARGB path and the DVD path of the
renderer (pgs_sat_wiring.py, fails on the tree before).

The idea is avdvplus's and pannal's (for CoreELEC) and CroqueMr's.
